      "content": "Dobbs v. Jackson (2022) presents a monumental shift from previous Court interpretations regarding the right to abortion. The majority opinion, written by Chief Justice Roberts, asserts that the right to an abortion is not an inherent liberty explicitly mentioned or alluded to in the Constitution as interpreted in Roe v. Wade and Planned Parenthood v. Casey. Instead, it concludes that the Constitution does not quietly accommodate the right to abortion and relinquishes the Court's authority to enshrine this right. The dissent, authored by Justice Ginsburg, criticizes this ruling as an unwarranted intrusion of the majority court into the unpleasant political and moral arena, profoundly disregarding the established norms of stare decisis and the privacy value logic celebrated in women's rights jurisprudence. Her view emphasizes the detrimental effects such decisions could have on women's bodies, as well as the Court's overstepping of its role, since the matter arguably pertains to legislative jurisdiction.",
